00:06:01.620 --> 00:06:07.680
on agent permission scoping. Everyone talks about giving agents tools as if access is a simple

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:14.100
on or off switch. In practice, permission models must encode task intent, temporal scope,

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:20.560
and blast radius limits simultaneously. The core mechanism starts with workload identity issued

00:06:20.560 --> 00:06:26.720
at deployment, rather than static service account keys. The identity carries only the minimal

00:06:26.720 --> 00:06:32.400
credentials required for the declared task and is revoked on completion. This adds a small

00:06:32.400 --> 00:06:37.660
issuance latency of tens of milliseconds but eliminates the long-lived credential surface

00:06:37.660 --> 00:06:43.460
that agents otherwise accumulate. When the agent attempts an action outside its declared scope,

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:49.520
the system can either deny or escalate. Most teams still default to human in the loop

00:06:49.520 --> 00:06:56.140
for everything which reintroduces friction and does not scale. The practical decision point is whether

00:06:56.140 --> 00:07:02.620
the task's blast radius justifies the extra round trip or whether scoped, expiring credentials are

00:07:02.620 --> 00:07:10.020
sufficient. The gacha that bites teams is assuming agents will self-limit exploration. Without explicit

00:07:10.020 --> 00:07:15.660
scoping, they will probe every available path exactly as described in recent enterprise security

00:07:15.660 --> 00:07:23.280
reporting. Test Melem 2 on multi-file refactoring tasks to see whether its routing improves consistency

